# Chapter 14 — Make Music

Your game moves and plays. Now give it a voice. Back in Chapter 8 you made single
sounds, and in Chapter 9 you found the beat — the steady pulse the whole console
counts on. Music is what you build *on top* of that pulse: notes at pitches,
notes stacked into chords, drums that hit on some beats and rest on others, and
all of it played together as a song.

You do not need to read sheet music to do this. In Motoi a note is just a number,
a chord is a little list of numbers, and a drum pattern is a row of ones and
zeros. If you can count and make a list, you can write music. Let us build a
tiny tune from the ground up.

## The beat sets the speed

Everything musical rides on the tempo from Chapter 9 — the beats per minute.
Before you play a single note, the console already knows how fast the pulse goes:

```motoi
(display (audio/tempo))
(newline)
```

```
120
```

`120` beats a minute — two beats a second, a comfortable walking pace. Every note
and drum hit you write from here lands on this pulse. Speed the tempo up and the
whole song plays faster; slow it down and it drags. The beat is the grid; the
music is what you place on it.

## A note is a number

A single note is one pitch. In Motoi a pitch is a number, and the friendly middle
of the keyboard — middle C — is `60`. Sound one note and look at what you made:

```motoi
(display (synth/chord 'lead '(60) 1 'mf))
(newline)
```

```
((:kind chord) (:section lead) (:pitches (60)) (:duration 1) (:dynamic mf))
```

One note, described in full: it is a `chord` on the `lead` voice, its `pitches`
are the single number `60` (middle C), it lasts `1` beat, and its loudness is
`mf` — *mezzo-forte*, medium loud. Every note you play is a little record like
this. `60` is middle C; `62` is the D just above it; every step up the keyboard is
`+1`. A melody is nothing but a walk up and down those numbers.

## A chord is notes stacked

Play several pitches at once and you get a chord. Hand `synth/chord` a *list* of
numbers instead of one, and they all sound together:

```motoi
(display (synth/chord 'strings '(60 64 67) 1 'mf))
(newline)
```

```
((:kind chord) (:section strings) (:pitches (60 64 67)) (:duration 1) (:dynamic mf))
```

Three pitches now — `60`, `64`, `67` — sounding together on the `strings` voice.
That is a C-major chord: `60` is C, `64` is E (four steps up), `67` is G (three
more). Stack those three and your ear hears *bright, happy, settled*. Change the
middle number to `63` and it turns minor — sadder. A chord is just a list of note
numbers, and the numbers you choose are the mood.

## A drum is a pattern of hits and rests

Drums do not have pitch; they have *rhythm*. You write a drum part as a row of
ones and zeros — `1` is a hit, `0` is a rest — and `synth/kit` reads it into a
pattern:

```motoi
(display (synth/kit 'acoustic \"1010\" 'kick))
(newline)
```

```
((:kind kit) (:name acoustic) (:steps 4) (:hits (0 2)) (:voice kick))
```

You wrote `\"1010\"` and the console read back a `4`-step pattern whose `hits`
land on steps `0` and `2` — the kick drum thumps on the first and third of every
four. That is the backbone of nearly every song you have ever danced to. Write
`\"1000\"` for a slow heartbeat, `\"1111\"` for a driving run, `\"1011\"` for
something with a stumble in it. The string *is* the rhythm; you can see the beat
just by reading the ones.

## A song is a score you play

A note, a chord, a drum — these are the words. A song is the sentence: a *list*
of note events handed to `synth/play`, which schedules them all at your tempo:

```motoi
(define song
  (list (synth/chord 'lead '(60) 1 'mf)
        (synth/chord 'lead '(64) 1 'mf)
        (synth/chord 'lead '(67) 1 'mf)))
(display (synth/play song 120))
(newline)
```

```
(ok ((:kind score) (:events 3) (:tempo 120)))
```

You built a three-note melody — C, E, G, climbing — collected it into a list
called `song`, and handed it to `synth/play`. The console answered `ok` and
described what it scheduled: a `score` of `3` events at tempo `120`. On a live
console those three notes would ring out, one per beat, a little rising phrase.
This is how a whole soundtrack is made: build the events, gather them in a list,
and play the list.

## What you just proved

You wrote music from numbers. You read the tempo the beat runs at (`120`), sounded
a single note and saw it is really pitch `60` — middle C, built a C-major chord by
stacking `60 64 67`, wrote a kick-drum rhythm as the string `\"1010\"` and watched
it become hits on steps `0` and `2`, and gathered three notes into a `song` that
`synth/play` scheduled as a `3`-event score. Every program ran and handed back a
real musical record you could read.

That is the last of the *making* — your game can now draw, move, respond, collide,
and sing. What is left is memory and shipping: a game that forgets everything the
moment you close it is a toy, not a game you come back to. The next chapter teaches
the console to *remember* — a high score, a saved level, the place you left off.

> **Tip**
>
> Keep pitches as plain numbers while you experiment — `60`, `62`, `64` — and
> nudge them by `+1` and `-1` to hear what each step does. Once a melody sounds
> right, the numbers ARE the sheet music: write them down and you can play the
> same tune again exactly. A song in Motoi is data, and data you can save, share,
> and change.

> **Warning**
>
> `synth/chord`, `synth/kit`, and `synth/play` build and *schedule* music — at
> the bare prompt they hand back the record that describes the sound, but no
> speaker is being driven here, so you read the music instead of hearing it. In a
> live console the beat loop from Chapter 9 pumps the schedule and the notes
> actually ring. What you see at the prompt is the honest score; the sound is what
> the running console makes of it. Appendix E covers the synth and audio DSP in
> full.

